  "question": {
    "text": "Let $\\alpha, \\beta$ be the roots of the equation $x^2-p x+r=0$ and $\\frac{\\alpha}{2}, 2 \\beta$ be the roots of the equation $x^2-q x+r=0$. Then, the value of $r$ is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "uAnxI",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{2}{9}(p-q)(2 q-p)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "PX6uM",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{2}{9}(q-p)(2 p-q)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "Tcw9W",
      "text": "\n$$\n\\frac{2}{9}(q-2 p)(2 q-p)\n$$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "ufsmq",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{2}{9}(2 p-q)(2 q-p)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "The equation $x^2-p x+r=0$ has roots $(\\alpha, \\beta)$ and the equation $x^2-q x+r=0$ has roots\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}, 2 \\beta\\right) . & \\\\\n\\Rightarrow & r=\\alpha \\beta \\text { and } \\alpha+\\beta=p \\text { and } \\frac{\\alpha}{2}+2 \\beta=q \\\\\n\\Rightarrow & \\beta=\\frac{2 q-p}{3} \\text { and } \\alpha=\\frac{2(2 p-q)}{3} \\\\\n\\Rightarrow & \\alpha \\beta=r=\\frac{2}{9}(2 q-p)(2 p-q)\n\\end{array}\n$$",
    "image": null
  },
